HERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';`\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a normal index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to read every user row and lowercase each email. The cost grows with the size of `users`, even though the filter returns about one row.\n\n2. **Scan of `orders` on `user_id`, if that column has no index.** Once the user is found, the join looks up that user's orders by `o.user_id`. Without an index on `orders.user_id`, that means a sequential scan of `orders` or a hash join that reads all of it. Postgres doesn't create an index on a foreign key automatically, so check whether one exists. I can't tell from this file.\n\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including `u.id` and `o.user_id` twice. That rules out index-only scans and sends extra data over the wire. Wide columns such as text or JSON make this worse.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**Index (preferred), keeping the query as is:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nUse `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` on a live Postgres table. This turns scan 1 into an index lookup and scan 2 into an index range scan.\n\n**Rewrite, if you can normalize emails on write:**\n```sql\n-- store emails lowercased (or use citext), then:\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.name\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org';\n```\nWith this version, a plain unique index on `users(email)` is enough. It's smaller and also enforces uniqueness. The explicit column list replaces `SELECT *`. I made up the column names, so replace them with the ones you need.\n\n## Caveats\n- I only have the query, not the schema, row counts, or `EXPLAIN` output. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the plan changes.\n- If the table is small, the planner may keep the sequential scan, and the indexes won't help.\n- The expression index only matches queries that use exactly `lower(email)`. If other code searches with `ILIKE` or a different expression, it won't use this index.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}